  "dom_id": "question-159507",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The scores on the final calculus test in states X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ points and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of scores in state X is smaller than the population mean of scores in state Y. They decide to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe scientists sampled $35$ students from state X and $23$ students from state Y, finding that their mean scores are $85$ points and $87$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-2\\pm 2.105.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
